@node emacsctl Commands
@section emacsctl Commands

@subsection Global flags

Accepted anywhere on the command line:

@table @option
@item -c, --context NAME
use a named context from the config file
@item --instance SEL
target instance: @code{<pid>}, @code{primary}, or @code{auto}
@item --host DEST
remote editor over ssh (@code{user@@machine})
@item -o, --output FMT
@code{table} (default), @code{json}, @code{yaml}, @code{raw}
@item -w, --watch
stream changes where supported (@code{logs})
@item --timeout SECS
RPC timeout (default 30)
@item --config PATH
config file (default @file{~/.config/cmacs/emacsctl.yaml}, or
@env{EMACSCTL_CONFIG})
@item -q, --quiet
suppress non-essential output
@item --version
client version
@item -h, --help
contextual help (see below)
@end table

Precedence: command-line flags > the selected context > config
@code{settings:} > built-in defaults.

@subsection Getting help

Help is contextual and available everywhere (generated through
GLib's option parser, so flag listings can never drift from the
parser): @code{emacsctl --help} for the global overview,
@code{emacsctl GROUP --help} for a group's subcommands,
@code{emacsctl CMD --help} for one command's flags plus the global
options, and @code{emacsctl help WORDS@dots{}} as an equivalent
spelling.  A bare group prints its subcommand listing and exits 2;
an unknown flag exits 2 with a @option{--help} hint; a typo'd verb
gets a did-you-mean suggestion.

@subsection Core commands

@table @code
@item instances
list running cmacs instances (pid, version, primary, uptime)
@item describe instance
full identity JSON: pid, version, features[], start time, MCP socket
@item eval [--lang L] EXPR...
evaluate in the editor; L: @code{elisp} (default), @code{crispy},
@code{bacon}, @code{eshell}
@item repl [--lang L]
interactive REPL (@pxref{emacsctl REPL})
@item logs [-f] [-n N]
tail @file{*Messages*}; @option{-f} follows live via the
@code{MessageLogged} signal
@item watch stream
stream BufferAdded/Removed + FrameAdded/Removed events
@item events [watch]
stream the live editor-events firehose
(@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Events}); see @ref{D-Bus Events}
@item events recent [N]
last N recorded events as JSON
@item events types
list the event taxonomy (@code{category/name})
@item config @dots{}
contexts (@pxref{emacsctl Contexts})
@item completion bash|zsh
shell completion script
@end table

@subsection Editor surface

Groups @code{get}, @code{buffer}, @code{edit}, @code{file},
@code{find-file}, @code{text} (whose verbs all take
@option{-b/--buffer NAME} and whose @code{insert}/@code{append} take @option{-e} to expand
backslash escapes like @command{echo -e} and read stdin when no
TEXT argument is given), @code{nav}, @code{search}, @code{vc},
@code{project}, @code{clip}, @code{bookmark}, @code{pkg},
@code{var}, @code{input}, @code{window}, @code{process},
@code{message}, @code{debug}, @code{c} (cintrospect/cpatch) and
@code{watch} cover the classic editing surface.  Run
@code{emacsctl help} for the live list with one-line summaries ---
the help output is generated from the same table that drives
dispatch, so it cannot go stale.

@subsection Subsystem groups

Available when the @emph{target} cmacs was built with the subsystem;
a missing subsystem maps to exit code 3 with a clear message:
@code{crispy}, @code{bacon}, @code{eshell}, @code{ai},
@code{brigade}, @code{gsurf},
@code{gnuseye}, @code{pod} (podomation), @code{video}, @code{audio},
@code{speech} (whisper + piper), @code{lrg} (libregnum editor),
@code{calc} (@code{eval}, @code{symbolic}, @code{units},
@code{list}, @code{describe}), @code{compositor} and @code{monitor}
(gowl), and @code{sb} (the second brain: @code{ingest}, @code{jobs},
@code{status}, @code{cancel}, @code{tree}, @code{find}, @code{doctor},
@code{open}, @code{layout}, @code{search}, @code{expand}, @code{node},
@code{stats}, @code{sources}, @code{refresh}; aliases
@code{second-brain} and @code{secondbrain}).

@subsection Agents: brigade spawn / brigade send / brigade log

Hands work to a brigade agent and then goes on talking to it.  The same
verbs an agent gets as MCP tools, so a shell script and a model drive the
queue through one code path.

@example
emacsctl brigade spawn "audit the config loader" reviewer
emacsctl brigade status 3f2a1c
emacsctl brigade result 3f2a1c
@end example

@command{send} continues the conversation rather than starting a new
one.  It works whether the agent is still running---the message is
delivered when its current turn ends---or finished long ago, in which
case it starts again on the same provider session and remembers
everything from before.

@example
emacsctl brigade send 3f2a1c "now check the error paths too"
emacsctl brigade inbox 3f2a1c
emacsctl brigade drop 3f2a1c 0
emacsctl brigade close 3f2a1c
@end example

@command{log} prints the whole transaction log---every message, reply and
tool call in order---for @emph{any} task, not only ones you started.
That is what makes it useful for inspecting an agent that is
misbehaving: @samp{emacsctl brigade log 3f2a1c 2} reads from turn two
onward.

A task whose worker has no session concept (@code{shell}) refuses
@command{send} rather than re-running with the follow-up as though it
were a first prompt.

@subsection AI: ai prompt / ai call / ai models / ai chat

The ai verbs take provider, model, and system prompt as flags
(@option{-p/--provider}, @option{-m/--model}, @option{-s/--system})
instead of positional slots.  @code{ai prompt} composes its text
from positionals, stdin, and @option{-f/--file} attachments
(repeatable; each file is appended with a @code{--- FILE: name ---}
header so the model can tell sources apart):

@example
emacsctl ai prompt 'why is the sky blue?'
emacsctl ai prompt -p ollama -m llama3.2 'one-liner: rsync a dir'
git diff | emacsctl ai prompt -s 'Review this diff for bugs'
emacsctl ai prompt -f main.c -f util.c 'find the bug'

emacsctl ai models                      # provider/model table, all
emacsctl ai models -p claude            # one provider
emacsctl ai models -p ollama -o json

emacsctl ai chat -p claude -m claude-opus-5 'hello'
@end example

@code{ai call} is like @code{ai prompt} (same @option{-p/-m/-s/-f}
flags, positionals, stdin) but adds @option{-t/--tools}: with it, the
model is given the built-in agent tools (bash/read/write/edit/glob/
grep/ls/web_fetch) and runs a synchronous multi-turn loop, returning
the final answer.

@example
emacsctl ai call 'summarise this in one line: ...'   # no tools
emacsctl ai call -t 'how many .c files are under src/, and why?'
@end example

@quotation Warning
A tool-enabled call runs the loop on the target's main thread; under
@command{emacs --gowl} that is the Wayland compositor, so a long loop
freezes the desktop.  Keep @option{-t} loops short, and prefer
@code{ai prompt} (no tools) when driving a live @option{--gowl}
session.
@end quotation

@code{ai models} renders a provider/model table; @option{-o json} /
@option{-o yaml} emit the provider-to-models map as structured data.
Without @option{-p} every provider is queried (API providers over
the network, CLI providers from static tables) and unavailable ones
report inline, so the call never fails outright --- but it can take
a while; @option{-p} keeps it fast.  The model names that
@code{ai models} prints are exactly what @option{-m/--model}
accepts.

The underlying typed methods are @code{Editor1.Ai} @code{Prompt} /
@code{Call} / @code{ListModels} / @code{OpenChat}, with MCP parity via
@code{ai_prompt} / @code{ai_call} / @code{ai_list_models} /
@code{ai_open_chat}.

@subsection Second brain: sb ingest / sb tree / sb find

The @code{sb} group is the notes repository from a shell --- the
@command{emacsctl} face of @code{cmacs-secondbrain-ingest}
(@pxref{Secondbrain Ingest}).  @code{second-brain}, @code{secondbrain}
and @code{second_brain} are aliases.

@example
emacsctl sb ingest https://youtu.be/dQw4w9WgXcQ            # captions -> summary -> note
emacsctl sb ingest -p resources -c technical/linux notes.pdf
emacsctl sb ingest -w -t "meeting,work" -T meeting call.mp3 # wait for the note
emacsctl sb ingest --crawl --depth 2 --max-pages 30 https://docs.example.com/
git log -5 | emacsctl sb ingest -f text --title "Recent commits" -p inbox
emacsctl sb ingest --dry-run -o json article.epub          # the plan, no work
emacsctl sb ingest -N -p inbox scan.pdf                    # no model at all

emacsctl sb jobs                                            # the queue
emacsctl sb status sbi-3
emacsctl sb tree -p resources -c technical                  # sbi --list
emacsctl sb find zfs snapshots                              # sbf
emacsctl sb doctor                                          # what this host can read
@end example

@code{sb ingest} queues jobs and returns their statuses at once; the
work runs on the editor's main loop.  @option{-w/--wait} polls
@code{IngestStatus} from the client until every job is done
(@option{--wait-timeout}, default 900 s).  Polling from the client
rather than blocking in the editor is deliberate: a @option{--gowl}
editor's main thread is the desktop.

Placement: @option{-p/--para} and @option{-c/--category}, or
@option{-d/--directory} for an exact root-relative directory.  Without
any, the editor's @code{cmacs-secondbrain-ingest-placement} decides ---
by default the model chooses from the real tree and falls back to the
inbox when unsure.

Text: @samp{-} as an input, @option{-i/--stdin}, or a pipe with no
positionals reads stdin; @option{-f/--format} names it.  Relative paths
are resolved in the shell's working directory, not the editor's.

The model: @option{-T/--type} picks the summary template,
@option{--prompt} appends instructions, @option{--principle} adds a
principles section, @option{-n/--no-summary} skips the summary,
@option{-N/--no-ai} uses no model at all, @option{-P/--provider} and
@option{-m/--model} override @code{claude-code} / @code{sonnet}.
@option{-s/--sanitize} redacts with the default rules;
@option{--sanitize-rules email,phone,bank-account} names rules.

Sites: @option{--crawl} turns a URL into a site directory with an index
and one note per page, bounded by @option{--depth}, @option{--max-pages},
@option{--include} and @option{--exclude}; robots.txt is honoured.
@option{--web gsurf} fetches through an offscreen WebKit view.

Other flags: @option{-t/--tags}, @option{--title}, @option{--name},
@option{-a/--append}, @option{-r/--recursive}, @option{--no-link},
@option{--keep-source}, @option{--root DIR}, @option{-b/--branch NAME}.

The underlying typed methods are @code{Editor1.SecondBrain}
@code{Ingest} / @code{IngestStatus} / @code{IngestList} /
@code{IngestCancel} / @code{Tree} / @code{Find} / @code{Doctor}, with MCP
parity via @code{secondbrain_ingest} and friends.

@subsection Org buffers: text insert-org

Org-aware insertion: target a headline (@option{-H} takes an exact
title at any depth, or a slash outline path like
@code{Projects/cmacs/Log}; @option{--create} makes missing
components), choose the spot inside the entry
(@option{-p top|bottom|subtree-end|point}; bottom --- the default ---
is the end of the entry's own body, before child headings), wrap the
text (@option{--quote}, @option{--src LANG}, @option{--example},
@option{--verse}, @option{--center}, @option{--block NAME},
@option{--drawer NAME}), or file it as a new child headline
(@option{--child TITLE} with optional @option{--todo} and
@option{--tags}).  @option{--timestamp} prepends an inactive org
timestamp; @option{-e} expands escapes; stdin feeds the text when no
argument is given.

@example
emacsctl text insert-org -b notes.org -H 'Projects/cmacs' \
  --src elisp '(message "hi")'
git log -1 | emacsctl text insert-org -b notes.org \
  -H 'Log/2026' --create --child "$(date +%F)" --todo TODO
@end example

Wrapping composes inside-out: block, then drawer, then the child
headline.  The underlying typed method is
@code{Editor1.Edit.InsertOrg} (options travel as an extensible
string dict), with MCP parity via the @code{insert_org} tool.

@subsection Org buffers: get content-org

@code{get content BUFFER} prints a buffer verbatim.  For org-mode
buffers, @code{get content-org} parses the document @emph{in the
editor} (org's own parser --- @code{org-map-entries}, planning info,
property drawers, the agenda match syntax) and returns a structured
tree, so @option{-o yaml} / @option{-o json} give you real data
instead of a text blob:

@example
emacsctl get content-org notes.org                # outline table
emacsctl get content-org notes.org -o yaml        # nested tree
emacsctl get content-org notes.org --match "/TODO" -o json
emacsctl get content-org notes.org --match "work+urgent" --depth 2
emacsctl get content-org notes.org --no-body --no-properties
@end example

@table @option
@item --match EXPR
org agenda match string: tags/todo/property queries like
@code{work+urgent/TODO} or @code{/DONE}
@item --depth N
limit headline depth
@item --no-body
structure only, drop entry body text
@item --no-properties
drop property drawers
@end table

Each headline carries @code{title}, @code{level}, @code{todo},
@code{priority}, @code{tags}, @code{scheduled} / @code{deadline} /
@code{closed}, @code{properties}, @code{body}, and @code{children};
the document carries @code{buffer}, @code{file}, and the
@code{#+TITLE} / @code{#+AUTHOR} / @code{#+DATE} / @code{#+FILETAGS}
keywords.  Keys are omitted when empty.  The default table output
renders an indented outline.

The underlying typed method is @code{Editor1.Edit.GetOrgContent},
with MCP parity via the @code{get_org_content} tool.

@subsection Output formats

@example
emacsctl get buffers                # aligned table (human)
emacsctl get buffers -o json        # pretty JSON array
emacsctl get buffers -o yaml        # YAML sequence
emacsctl get buffers -o raw         # one name per line, stable
emacsctl eval '(+ 1 2)' -o raw      # "3" --- byte-exact, no newline
@end example

@subsection Examples

@example
# Save every modified buffer whose name ends in .org:
emacsctl get buffers -o raw \
  | grep '\.org$' \
  | while read -r b; do emacsctl buffer save "$b"; done

# Editor health one-liner for a status bar:
emacsctl describe instance -o json | jq -r .uptime

# Hot-patch a C primitive, then verify:
emacsctl c patch some-defun my_replacement_fn
emacsctl c patch-log
@end example
